{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01873XKTUvdUGG3wmiQe1i3Y","type":"tool_result","content":"===== Render fails (exit 1, unnamed internal error) on small square canvases when the theme list cannot fit\nEVIDENCE: themeBlock shrinks px to 6 (line 523: `while (px > 6 && ...) px--`) but never checks that the block then fits. Lines run past the strip and placeInsetPieces throws. `m0saic make ... -w 300 -h 300 --props @journal/2026-09-29/review/stress/8long.props.json` (a 21x21 with themes 1A 1D 19D 127A, 21 letters each) prints 'Error: placeInsetRects: rects[17] (y=293, h=10) overflows rootH=300' and exits 1. The square panel there is 40 px tall, and theme-3 is placed at y=293 with h=10. The scan also throws at 240x240, 270x270, 300x270, 320x300, 360x300 and for a 3x25 with 8 themes at 240-270 wide. Every canvas with both sides at least 400 px is clean, and all 7 contract canvases plus 1200x630 and 1080x1350 are clean, so this is outside the contract.\nREPRO: node C:/src/m0saic-production/one-a-day/journal/2026-09-29/review/stress/scan.cjs | grep THROW\nFIX: When the list cannot fit at 6 px, fail fast with a named message (canvas too small for N theme answers), or drop to fewer lines. Don't let the engine throw an anonymous rect overflow.\n===== A themeless puzzle (themeEntries: \"\") or the teaser leaves 75% of H empty in the landscape panel and about 25% of H empty under the portrait board\nEVIDENCE: The landscape and portrait branches reserve the list's band whether or not a list exists. Landscape (lines 562-564) pins the title and byline to the top and the meta line to the bottom. Portrait (line 568: `headH = Math.round(rest * 0.36)`) sizes the header and board the same with or without a list. Measured on the current dist, the text-free band between byline and meta (landscape) or between board and meta (portrait) is: 1920x1080 811 px (75% of H), 1200x630 472 px (75%), 1080x1920 474 px (25%), 1080x1350 321 px (24%). It is the same for {solved:false}. Real renders, exit 0: review/stress/v2-themeless-1920x1080.png (title and byline in the top 190 px, then empty panel to the meta line at y=1003), review/stress/themeless-1080x1920.png (about 460 px empty under the board), review/stress/teaser-1920x1080.png. Themeless grids are routine (Fri/Sat puzzles), and the brief's rubric 1 asks landscape and portrait to 'use their extra space on purpose (no empty half-panel)'. The layout brief also says the landscape column must not be 'a top-heavy stack over empty space'.\nREPRO: echo '{\"themeEntries\": \"\"}' > journal/2026-09-29/review/stress/themeless.props.json && m0saic make @one-a-day/gaming/crossword-grid-card/v1 --template-repo . -w 1920 -h 1080 --props @journal/2026-09-29/review/stress/themeless.props.json -o journal/2026-09-29/review/stress/themeless.png\nFIX: When there are no theme lines, spread the title/byline and meta across the column instead: centre the title block vertically, or let the title grow. In portrait, rebalance headH and the board size so the footer does not keep an empty list band. For the teaser this conflicts with the brief's 'nothing else moves', so at least handle themeEntries \"\" and write the teaser trade-off in 30-build.md.\nVERDICT: medium I reproduced the claim on the current build. dist (11:22) is newer than src (11:21) and contains the same layout lines: `free0 = by + ...` and `headH = Math.round(rest * 0.36)`.\n\n**What the code does:**\n- **Landscape** (src lines 554-564): the title and byline are pinned to the panel top and the meta line to the panel bottom. `themeBlock` returns 0 when `showThemes` is false, so nothing fills the band between them.\n- **Portrait** (lines 566-587): `headH` and the board position come from `rest` alone, and they are the same whether or not a list exists. The meta line is pinned to the bottom, so with no list the whole footer band stays empty.\n\n**Renders** (all exit 0, in `review/verify/emptyband/`):\n- `themeless-1920x1080.png`: \"Cats and Dogs\" and the byline sit in the top ~190 px of the righ\n===== No contrast guard on themeColor: a dark colour hides theme letters, numbers and rings, and makes theme cells read as blocks\nEVIDENCE: themeColor is only checked against /^#[0-9a-fA-F]{6}$/ (line 282). The schema offers a free colour picker. Letters, numbers and rings are always INK #15171C drawn over the fill. WCAG contrast of ink on fill: #FFE08A (default) 13.9:1; #1E3A8A (navy) 1.73:1; #0000FF 2.09:1; #6A1B9A (purple) 1.91:1; #008000 3.49:1; #D32F2F 3.60:1; #15171C 1.00:1, where theme cells are identical to black squares. Real render review/stress/v2-darktheme-1080x1080.png ({\"themeColor\":\"#1E3A8A\"}, exit 0): DOGSLED/CATNAPS letters barely visible, clue numbers 9/10/12-15 and the rings nearly invisible, and the rules between theme cells disappear.\nREPRO: echo '{\"themeColor\": \"#1E3A8A\"}' > journal/2026-09-29/review/stress/darktheme.props.json && m0saic make @one-a-day/gaming/crossword-grid-card/v1 --template-repo . -w 1080 -h 1080 --props @journal/2026-09-29/review/stress/darktheme.props.json -o journal/2026-09-29/review/stress/dark.png\nFIX: Fail fast in normalizeCrossword when contrast(INK, themeColor) < 4.5 (the brief's own bar), with the field named, for example 'themeColor #1E3A8A is too dark for ink letters (1.7:1, needs 4.5:1)'. Or state the limit in the prop description and caveats.\nVERDICT: low I reproduced it. The code does what the claim says. normalizeCrossword only checks themeColor against HEX (/^#[0-9a-fA-F]{6}$/, line 282). drawCrosswordCard always draws the letters, numbers and rings in INK #15171C over the themeColor tint (layers 2, 4, 6-8). The schema also offers a free colorPicker. My own script (review/verify/darktheme/contrast.cjs), which requires the built dist, shows normalizeCrossword accepts #1E3A8A, #15171C and #000000 unchanged. The claim's contrast figures are correct (navy 1.73, #0000FF 2.09, purple 1.91, green 3.49, red 3.60, default 13.90).\n\nWhat I saw in my renders (all exit 0):\n- navy-1080.png: the DOGSLED/CATNAPS letters can still be read at the hint size, so \"barely visible\" overstates it. Clue numbers 9, 10 and 12-15, the rings and the rules between th\n===== 48-char author of wide glyphs drops the byline to 7 px at 480x270 (brief: no panel text under 8 px at 480x270)\nEVIDENCE: pairFit falls through to its sub-floor range (line 375: `[6, floorPx - 1]`) when two lines at 8 px don't fit. layoutCrosswordCard on the current dist at 480x270 with author 'W'.repeat(48) or 'M'.repeat(48) gives byline-by 7 px and byline 7 px, 2 lines. Realistic authors are fine: 'constructorwithareallylonghandlefromcrosshare123' (48, no spaces) and the test's spaced 48-char author stay at 8 px or more. The test's 8 px assertion only uses spaced copy. Real render review/stress/authorW48-480x270.png, exit 0.\nREPRO: node C:/src/m0saic-production/one-a-day/journal/2026-09-29/review/stress/sweep2.cjs author-W48   (see the @480x270 UNDER8 lines)\nFIX: Allow a third byline line at the 8 px floor before going under it. Or accept this edge and state that the 8 px floor holds for ordinary text only.\n===== A grid with no entries passes validation but stamps a cell-number constraint on the string '-Infinity' for a label that is never drawn\nEVIDENCE: grid 'A#A/###/A#A' passes parseCrosswordGrid (it has white cells). numbers is empty, so `String(Math.max(...p.numbers.values()))` is \"-Infinity\". Because numberPx > 0, a textFits constraint for 'cell-number' is pushed, but no cell-number source exists (nums is empty). With debugLayout:true the render reports layoutContract ok:false, 'cell-number: missing-label'. This is an implausible grid and the render itself does not crash.\nREPRO: node C:/src/m0saic-production/one-a-day/journal/2026-09-29/review/correctness/06-edge-renders.cjs (first case)\nFIX: Push the cell-number constraint only when nums.length > 0 (p.numbers.size > 0), or refuse a grid with no entries in parseCrosswordGrid with a named message.\n===== The teaser still re-fits the caption line with long copy ('nothing else moves' holds only for the defaults)\nEVIDENCE: With max-length title, author and publication at 1080x1080, going from solved:true to solved:false changes meta-publication from 21 px to 22 px, moves meta-date x from 294 to 306 and moves the tail x from 425 to 443. '| 7x7 | puzzle' is narrower than '| 7x7 | solution', so metaFit lands on a different size. The new 'nothing else moves' test covers only the default props. Title and byline no longer move after the leftW fix.\nREPRO: node C:/src/m0saic-production/one-a-day/journal/2026-09-29/review/correctness/05-teaser-and-cellfit.cjs (the 'max copy @ 1080x1080' block)\nFIX: Fit the meta line on the wider of the two tails (measure with 'solution' in both modes), then draw the actual tail. Extend the test to the max-copy props.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fadc3ba2-c902-457a-8c6b-b57eed4efb97","uuid":"656ea175-56f8-45f9-a611-c6312c22bfc1","timestamp":"2026-09-29T15:29:42.325Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"===== Render fails (exit 1, unnamed internal error) on small square canvases when the theme list cannot fit\nEVIDENCE: themeBlock shrinks px to 6 (line 523: `while (px > 6 && ...) px--`) but never checks that the block then fits. Lines run past the strip and placeInsetPieces throws. `m0saic make ... -w 300 -h 300 --props @journal/2026-09-29/review/stress/8long.props.json` (a 21x21 with themes 1A 1D 19D 127A, 21 letters each) prints 'Error: placeInsetRects: rects[17] (y=293, h=10) overflows rootH=300' and exits 1. The square panel there is 40 px tall, and theme-3 is placed at y=293 with h=10. The scan also throws at 240x240, 270x270, 300x270, 320x300, 360x300 and for a 3x25 with 8 themes at 240-270 wide. Every canvas with both sides at least 400 px is clean, and all 7 contract canvases plus 1200x630 and 1080x1350 are clean, so this is outside the contract.\nREPRO: node C:/src/m0saic-production/on